Atrophic Vaginitis
Obstetrics Gynaecology & Womens health
Vaginal dryness, Pale non-rugated vaginal mucosa, Dyspareunia/postcoital spotting

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: OGW-003
Reason for Visit: Acute urinary retention and severe, burning vulvar pain causing inability to void and distress.
Location: Nursing home
Present at Visit: Patient and nursing staff
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated acute inability to pass urine secondary to severe localized pain and burning, risking bladder distension. Urgent after-hours assessment was required to evaluate for urinary tract infection versus severe mucosal atrophy, manage pain, and ensure bladder emptying to prevent renal injury before the next in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: It burns so badly down there that I absolutely cannot pass urine, and there is blood on the tissue.
History of Present Illness: The patient reports a progressive history of severe vaginal and vulvar dryness causing significant daily discomfort over the past year. Earlier this evening, she attempted to void but experienced extreme burning irritation and dysuria, leading to acute urinary hesitancy. She notes minor postcoital spotting previously but tonight noticed light vaginal bleeding from fragile mucosa upon wiping. She also reports a history of dyspareunia specifically characterized by inadequate lubrication and tearing pain, though she is not currently active. Pruritus has been constant for weeks.
Functional Status: Requires mobility assistance; currently distressed and avoiding movement due to vulvar pain.
Social/Safety Concerns: High risk of recurrent urinary tract infections and potential bladder distension if unable to void.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 135/85 mmHg | HR: 92 bpm | Temp: 37.1 C | RR: 18 bpm | SpO2: 97% | Wt: 65 Kg
General: Patient is visibly uncomfortable, shifting in the bed and anxious about needing to urinate.
Physical Exam: Vulvar examination reveals loss of labial fat pad fullness and retraction of the introitus. The visible vaginal vault walls are pale, thin, shiny, and non-rugated. Petechiae and easily friable tissue are noted with minor manipulation. Mild suprapubic fullness palpated. Urinalysis via gentle in-and-out catheterization shows trace blood but is negative for nitrites and leukocytes.
Environmental Observations: Call bell within reach, bathroom easily accessible with grab bars.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against nursing home chart; taking daily calcium and vitamin D. No active topical treatments.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Atrophic Vaginitis (Genitourinary Syndrome of Menopause) with acute dysuria - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Acute Bacterial Cystitis - Unlikely due to negative nitrites and leukocyte esterase on urinalysis.

Vulvar Carcinoma - Unlikely due to the generalized, diffuse nature of the mucosal thinning without discrete ulcerated or raised lesions.

Candidal Vulvovaginitis - Unlikely given the absence of thick, white, curdy discharge and the presence of pale, thin mucosa rather than erythematous inflammation.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented with acute inability to void secondary to severe vulvovaginal burning and dysuria, complicated by light bleeding. Objective findings confirmed severe mucosal atrophy, friable tissue, and mild suprapubic fullness indicative of impending urinary retention. The clinical severity of the pain and the risk of bladder distension required urgent after-hours catheterization to decompress the bladder and immediate topical intervention. This assessment and management could not be safely delayed until the next in-hours period without risking acute urinary retention and escalating distress, fulfilling MBS criteria for urgent evaluation.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Performed gentle in-and-out catheterization yielding 400mL of clear urine, instantly relieving suprapubic pressure. Applied a soothing, bland barrier cream (zinc oxide base) to the vulvar region to protect fragile tissue from urine. Prescribed topical vaginal estrogen cream to commence tomorrow.
Positive Behavioral Change: To reduce stinging when passing urine, pour a jug of warm water over the vulva while voiding; the immediate reduction in burning will reinforce the habit of protecting the sensitive mucosa. To restore tissue health, apply the prescribed estrogen cream strictly as directed; experiencing the gradual return of comfort will encourage consistent adherence to the treatment plan.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Nursing staff instructed on barrier cream application.
DME/Safety (Optional): Not applicable.
Follow-up: Routine GP review in one week to assess response to topical estrogen therapy.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Nursing staff to transfer to the Emergency Department if the patient becomes completely unable to pass urine for more than 6 hours again, develops a fever over 38.0 C, or experiences heavy vaginal bleeding.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is presumptive based on clinical signs; long-term management requires regular review.
Clinical Course: The extreme burning should improve with the barrier cream and proper voiding techniques. Tissue thickness and moisture will slowly improve over several weeks with estrogen cream.
SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Inform the nursing staff immediately if you cannot pass urine, if the burning worsens significantly, or if you feel feverish.
PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The extreme dryness and thinning of your tissues caused microscopic tears that sting severely when urine touches them. The estrogen cream will slowly rebuild the thickness and moisture of these tissues.
Actionable Steps: Use the warm water pouring technique every time you urinate, and ensure the nurses apply the barrier cream after drying.

